{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["The meaning of <b>he who offers it shall not have it reckoned.</b> After the fat portions are offered up on high, all that remains is holy; likewise the stones of the altar — after they are consecrated it is not fitting that any of them remain for profane use; therefore whole stones." "<b>Abomination.</b> As the Aramaic translator rendered it; likewise 'and the broth of their abominable things' (Isaiah 65:4). Some ask: after it went up as a pleasing fragrance, how is it not reckoned? The reason concerns intention, for the nature of the shelamim is to be holy, and the abominable thing is not holy. Furthermore, because he did not keep the commandment, his reward is lost through his loss — if it is shelamim, and if it is a vow, a new liability for sin is incurred corresponding to the first, if he does not fulfill his vow." "<b>Shall bear his penalty.</b> Punishment, but Scripture did not state 'shall be cut off' concerning one who is impure who eats consecrated meat."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:08:59.995740768Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}